Add a Missing metadata dashboard and progress bars for every bulk action

New services/metadata_health.py aggregates what's still missing across the
four enrichment sources - album artwork, artist bios, track popularity and
lyrics - as display rows, and MissingMetadataDialog browses them one tab per
source. Picking a row jumps to the artist/release page where the existing fix
button already lives; the dashboard itself stays browse-and-jump only.

The scan runs on MetadataScanThread with all four phases inside it, so the
progress bar moves from the first tick rather than sitting silent through the
three "fast" queries. The result is cached in SettingsView._last_metadata_scan
so reopening after fixing one item is instant, visited rows are marked across
reopens, and a bulk fix (bios/popularity/artwork) or the dialog's own Rescan
button invalidates the cache.

Artist image import, Verify files and chart re-matching were still running
synchronously on the UI thread with no feedback; each now has its own thread
(ArtistImagesImportThread / VerifyFilesThread / RematchChartsThread) and real
progress plumbed through the services behind it - import_artist_images,
mark_missing_files, mark_missing_videos and rematch_chart all take an optional
progress callback. SettingsView.rematch_all's loop over external charts moved
down into charts.rematch_all_charts so the thread has a service-level entry
point, matching the other bulk actions.

With nine background jobs to cross-check, each handler's own chain of "is
something else running" checks collapsed into one shared _busy_with().

An artist or release opened from the dashboard gets a "Missing metadata"
leading breadcrumb back to it, via three new AppContext signals rather than
Library and Settings talking to each other directly. ArtistDetailPanel.
set_artist takes a root_label and LibraryView._open_artist a root action, both
defaulting to the Artists grid so every existing caller is unchanged.

Settings rows reordered: Missing metadata first, next to the progress bar it
drives; the Last.fm API key and Discogs token rows moved to the bottom as a
pair, just above Nuke library.

